{
  "ranking": [
    {
      "id": "patch_1d01c9aac1b0",
      "failure_port": "o_wb_ack",
      "source_line": 471,
      "mechanism": "Reverses byte order when loading WB write data into r_data_reg. The public trace shows the initial o_wb_ack=1 at cycle 6 when the WB ack should remain low until a qualified bus cycle. This patch alters the data register contents that drive cmd_state transitions which gate o_wb_ack through r_cmd_busy/q_busy logic. The first mismatch cycle 6 shows actual ack=1 vs expected 0, indicating spurious acknowledgment from incorrect data sequencing."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_4dd6c52c7c48",
      "failure_port": "o_wb_ack",
      "source_line": 575,
      "mechanism": "Reverses r_data_reg byte order before driving o_wb_data. While o_wb_data is not the failing port, the reversed register feeds back into cmd_state machine (r_cmd_state transitions at line 9010) which controls r_cmd_busy and thus o_wb_ack through the q_busy pipeline. Cycle 6 mismatch (ack=1 vs 0) corresponds to first WB transaction where byte-swapped data would cause premature cmd completion."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_c1fed5d49f35",
      "failure_port": "o_wb_ack",
      "source_line": 300,
      "mechanism": "Changes cmd_state==0 guard from unqualified to require pre_cmd_state. pre_cmd_state is asserted when ll_cmd_stb&&ll_idle (line 8623), which occurs after low-level SPI accepts a command byte. The original unqualified path allows cmd_state to advance immediately on new_cmd, but the trace expects ack to stay low until bus cycle qualification. Cycle 6 ack=1 (expected 0) matches premature cmd_state advancement."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_84188b04a639",
      "failure_port": "o_wb_ack",
      "source_line": 300,
      "mechanism": "Qualifies cmd_state==0 transition with !ll_cmd_stb. Since ll_cmd_stb goes high when low-level accepts first command byte, this blocks cmd_state advancement until SPI goes idle. The trace shows ack asserting at cycle 6 (first WB write) but expected 0, indicating cmd_state should not advance until later cycle. This patch delays advancement, aligning with expected ack=0 at cycle 6."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_2e7dcb4295ae",
      "failure_port": "o_wb_ack",
      "source_line": 324,
      "mechanism": "Guards cmd_state==5 (FIFO write path) with r_use_fifo. The WB write at cycle 6 has wb_data[11]=0 (r_use_fifo=0), so this would block the FIFO write transition. However trace shows ack=1 at cycle 6, meaning cmd_state advanced past 5 incorrectly. This patch prevents that advancement when FIFO not used, keeping r_cmd_busy high longer and ack low."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_9ce4a09dbd52",
      "failure_port": "o_wb_ack",
      "source_line": 291,
      "mechanism": "Qualifies pre_cmd_state with ll_cmd_stb. pre_cmd_state gates the entire cmd_state machine (line 8623). Original pre_cmd_state = ll_cmd_stb&&ll_idle; this change makes it ll_cmd_stb&&ll_idle&&ll_cmd_stb (redundant). But the trace mismatch at cycle 6 suggests pre_cmd_state timing is wrong; this patch doesn't change timing since ll_cmd_stb is already in the AND."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_05776958ee91",
      "failure_port": "o_wb_ack",
      "source_line": 337,
      "mechanism": "Changes cmd_state==6 (FIFO read completion wait) to require r_fifo_wr. At cycle 6, r_fifo_wr=0 (wb_data[10]=0), so this would block exit from state 6. However cmd_state==6 is reached after state 5 which requires r_use_fifo=1, but cycle 6 write has r_use_fifo=0. This patch affects later cycles, not the first ack mismatch at cycle 6."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_5885b85d4068",
      "failure_port": "o_wb_ack",
      "source_line": 329,
      "mechanism": "Qualifies r_use_fifo condition with !r_have_resp. This gates second_rsp_state assignment (line 9810) which controls RSP state machine feeding r_rsp_state. The ack mismatch at cycle 6 is too early for RSP machine involvement; RSP starts after cmd_state reaches 5 and response received. This patch affects later ack cycles (7, 11, etc.) not cycle 6."
    }
  ]
  ,
  "explanation": "The primary failure is o_wb_ack asserting 1 at cycle 6 when expected 0. This indicates the WB acknowledgment pipeline fires prematurely. The acknowledgment is driven by wb_stb (line 583) which equals i_wb_stb&&!o_wb_stall. The trace input shows i_wb_stb=1 at cycle 6 with i_wb_cyc=1, i_wb_we=1, i_wb_addr=0 (CMD), i_wb_data=128. The cmd_state machine must not complete this command until later cycles. Patches that delay cmd_state advancement (c1fed5d49f35, 84188b04a639, 2e7dcb4295ae) directly address this by adding guards to early cmd_state transitions. Byte-order patches (1d01c9aac1b0, 4dd6c52c7c48) indirectly affect cmd_state via r_data_reg contents. The RSP-related patches (5885b85d4068, 05776958ee91) operate too late in the pipeline to explain cycle 6 mismatch. patch_9ce4a09dbd52 is structurally redundant."
}
